// #1511 / #2121 / #2144: the `handle` eligibility boundary, and the one row of
// this corpus whose verdict depends on which entry the compile names.
effect Ask { Get() -> Int }

fn ask_once() -> Int with Ask { perform Ask::Get() }

// The callee is a rowless LOCAL CLOSURE bound outside the handled body, which
// is the shape the eligibility judgement rejects (ADR-0076's effect-lowering
// prelude): nothing in scope tells it whether calling `bump` performs `Ask`.
//
// It is recorded here as `ok` for the `__no_entry__` lane and `reject` for the
// entry lane (fixtures/typecheck/expected.tsv columns 4-5), because that is
// exactly what it does. `__no_entry__` builds a test runner, `main` is never
// lowered, and the judgement never runs; with entry `main` -- what `vibe check`
// and `vibe build` do -- the program is rejected with:
//
//   handle of effect 'Ask' cannot be compiled here: this handle cannot see what
//   one call in its body performs (here: the call to 'bump'). Make that call
//   visible -- declare 'bump' as a top-level `fn`, give the binding or
//   parameter it arrives through an effect row (`with Ask`), or move its `let`
//   inside the handled body.
//
// An earlier version of this comment said "measured, it compiles", full stop,
// and was used to argue that the rejected example in book/en/13_effects.vibe.md
// was wrong. It had been measured through the no-entry lane only. The book is
// right about programs; this file is right about the lane -- which is why the
// row now records both verdicts instead of one (#2142, #2144).
//
// The blind spot is code that only `main` reaches: measured, this same shape
// written inside a `test` block is rejected in the no-entry lane too, because
// the synthesized runner reaches it. That is why
// handle_eligibility_diagnostic_test.vibe can measure the boundary with
// `__no_entry__` and still be right.
//
// The rest of the boundary -- every accepted shape, and the message each
// rejected one produces -- is pinned by
// lib/@vibe/compiler/tests/handle_eligibility_diagnostic_test.vibe (#2137).
fn main() -> Unit {
  let bump = (n: Int) -> Int { n + 1 }
  let _ = handle { bump(ask_once()) } with { Ask::Get() => resume(1) }
}
